    "https://careers.msu.edu/jobs/assistant-associate-full-professor-tenure-system-east-lansing-michigan-united-states-36bb285b-e614-4bc2-810d-eca435ee6ce7": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Tenure System",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Tenure System - East Lansing, Michigan, United States Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Tenure System - East Lansing, Michigan, United States Kiosk mode Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Tenure System Radiology Human Medicine 10022711 Faculty/Academic Staff Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Tenure System East Lansing, Michigan, United States Radiology Human Medicine 10022711 Area of Interest: Research/Scientific Full Time/Part Time: Full Time (90-100%) Group: Tenure System Faculty Remote Work: Remote-Friendly Union/Non-Union: Union Show More Show Less Faculty/Academic Staff Opening on: Oct 1 2026 Closing at: Oct 1 2028 - 23:55 EDT Salary Commensurate with Experience College Of Human Medicine 1166963 Add to favorites View favorites Working/Functional Title Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Tenure System Position Summary Michigan State University's Department of Radiology invites applications for tenure-system faculty at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor and/or Professor to join the Center for Imaging and Image-Guided Therapy (CIIGT). Successful candidates will contribute to the scientific growth and national prominence of the CIIGT, a state-of-the-art translational imaging center equipped with a Siemens Biograph One PET/MRI system, advanced radiopharmacy capabilities, and extensive preclinical ( https://imaging.iq.msu.edu/ ) and clinical imaging resources ( https://biomedicalimaging.msu.edu ). They will interact with our Precision Health Program (PHP; https://radiology.msu.edu/research/precision-health-program ) and the Institute for Quantitative Health Sciences and Engineering ( https://iq.msu.edu/ ) to advance new technologies aimed at improving health. The CIIGT harnesses the full potential of Michigan State University’s unique research and clinical assets. With two co-located medical colleges, a college of nursing, a college of veterinary medicine, nationally recognized programs in animal and agricultural sciences and our world-renowned Facility for Rare Isotope Beams ( Facility for Rare Isotope Beams ), the Institute for Quantitative Health Sciences and Engineering, and clinical imaging research ( https://izzofamilymedicalcenter.com/) , combined with established and emerging clinical partnerships across the state, CIIGT is a truly distinctive research environment, unlike any other in the world. CIIGT is an inspiring and tangible manifestation of MSU’s One Team, One Health initiative envisioned to bring our strengths in human, animal and environmental health together to become a national powerhouse of precision health and healing. The CIIGT provides a collaborative nexus of innovation uniquely enabling scientists to leverage human and large animal models and reciprocal translation to the benefit of all life. This position is part of Michigan State University’s Global Impact Initiative (GII), a comprehensive strategic investment in new and enhanced research endeavors designed to advance big ideas, innovation, and global impact while responding to the MSU 2030 Strategic Plan. The GII supports faculty recruitment in high-priority areas of research and aligns investments with university and college priorities, historical strengths, and new opportunities for growth. As part of this strategic investment, faculty members will bring or develop innovative, externally funded research programs and work closely with the CIIGT Director and investigators across the Colleges of Human Medicine, Osteopathic Medicine, Veterinary Medicine, Engineering, and other university partners to advance translational imaging research spanning human and animal health. Please note that this is a union position represented by the Union of Tenure System Faculty (UTSF). The university is working with the union to establish a collective bargaining agreement. As an institution of higher learning, Michigan State University is committed to providing a safe environment for its students, faculty, and staff in support of its educational mission. With this commitment, the University conducts criminal background checks and professional misconduct reviews of all applicants for employment in faculty, academic staff, and executive management searches. Professional Misconduct Review A professional misconduct review is a prerequisite for a candidate to be selected for this position. Applicants will be asked to disclose whether they are subject to any pending investigation, findings or sanctions related to professional misconduct. Professional misconduct includes misconduct related to a person’s employment, including, but not limited to, theft, embezzlement, research integrity, discrimination, or harassment under civil rights laws and policies, including protected identity harassment, sexual harassment, sexual misconduct (sexual assault, sexual exploitation, dating violence, domestic violence, stalking, etc.), and retaliation. All applicants will be asked to sign an authorization and release, which authorizes the University to contact the candidate’s current and former employers, for a period of 7 years prior to the date of application, related to any pending investigations, findings of responsibility and/or sanctions related to professional misconduct. Nothing will be sent to current or former employers unless the candidate reaches the semi-finalist stage. The existence of professional misconduct history does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment. The University will assess the history, including any information provided by the candidate, in determining whether it is compatible with the position. The University may decline to hire a candidate based on the professional misconduct review. All records obtained from external employers will be kept in a secure location, separate from personnel files. If an applicant fails to sign the authorization and release, the application will be deemed incomplete and will be withdrawn. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, citizenship, age, disability or protected veteran status. Required Degree Terminal -imaging science, radiology, biomedical engineering, medical physics, nuclear medicine, neuroscience, cancer biology, radiopharmaceutical sciences, or a related discipline Minimum Requirements Appropriate doctoral degree (PhD, MD/DO, DVM, or equivalent) in imaging science, radiology, biomedical engineering, medical physics, nuclear medicine, neuroscience, cancer biology, radiopharmaceutical sciences, or a related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in PET imaging, MRI, or molecular imaging and experience applying PET, PET/MRI, PET/CT, or related radiopharmaceutical/imaging approaches to important scientific and/or clinical questions. Relevant expertise may include quantitative image analysis, kinetic modeling, radiotracer development, dosimetry, image-guided applications, or other advanced imaging methods. For the proposed rank: evidence of independence and a trajectory appropriate to the candidate's career stage, including success in obtaining, or strong potential to obtain, competitive extramural funding. Assistant Professor candidates should demonstrate an emerging independent program and strong funding potential; Associate Professor candidates should demonstrate an established independent program and active extramural support; Professor candidates should demonstrate a sustained, significant record of independent extramural support and research leadership. Desired Qualifications Strong record of mentoring students, trainees, and early-career investigators. Experience in translational science, image-guided therapies, and/or clinical trials. Evidence of interdisciplinary research and team building. Required Application Materials Cover letter summarizing your qualifications and interest Curriculum vitae, including a list of publications and funding history A vision statement for research (3 page maximum) Names and contact information for at least three professional references Special Instructions Proof of license and/or certifications may be requested during the interview process. Application review begins November 1, 2026; Applications will be accepted until the positions are filled. Review of Applications Begins On 11/01/2026 Summary of Health Risks exposure to ionizing radiation and radiopharmaceuticals Remote Work Statement MSU strives to provide a flexible work environment and this position has been designated as remote-friendly. Remote-friendly means some or all of the duties can be performed remotely as mutually agreed upon. Website www.radiology.msu.edu MSU Statement Michigan State University has been advancing the common good with uncommon will for more than 160 years. One of the top research universities in the world, MSU pushes the boundaries of discovery and forges enduring partnerships to solve the most pressing global challenges while providing life-changing opportunities to a diverse and inclusive academic community through more than 200 programs of study in 17 degree-granting colleges. Apply Now Not Quite What You're Looking For? Sign Up For Job Alerts First Name (required) 48dc9c11 Last Name (required) b5b760e9 Email (required) afc7872b Job Alert f0d03eef Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Radiology Human Medicine 10022711 Radiology Human Medicine 10022711 Categories Area of Interest: Research/Scientific Full Time/Part Time: Full Time (90-100%) Group: Tenure System Faculty Remote Work: Remote-Friendly Union/Non-Union: Union Area of Interest: Research/Scientific Full Time/Part Time: Full Time (90-100%) Group: Tenure System Faculty 