    "https://careers.msu.edu/jobs/postdoctoral-scholar-fixed-term-east-lansing-michigan-united-states-a1eec9dc-ddb9-4658-9636-314188410991": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar-Fixed Term",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Scholar-Fixed Term - East Lansing, Michigan, United States Postdoctoral Scholar-Fixed Term - East Lansing, Michigan, United States Kiosk mode Postdoctoral Scholar-Fixed Term Fisheries And Wildlife 10002344 Faculty/Academic Staff Postdoctoral Scholar-Fixed Term East Lansing, Michigan, United States Fisheries And Wildlife 10002344 Area of Interest: Research/Scientific Full Time/Part Time: Full Time (90-100%) Group: Fixed Term Academic Staff Remote Work: Remote-Friendly Union/Non-Union: Non-Union Show More Show Less Faculty/Academic Staff Opening on: Jul 30 2026 Closing at: Jul 30 2028 - 23:55 EDT 56000.00 College Of Agriculture And Nat Resources 1149929 Add to favorites View favorites Position Summary A 1.5-year Postdoctoral Researcher position is available working with Drs. Latimore and Cheruvelil in the Department of Fisheries and Wildlife at Michigan State University. The successful candidate will join the Bridge Lab to conduct data-intensive and collaborative lake and stream ecology research and translate results into action in partnership with communities. The postdoc will work on two funded projects: Turning MiCorps community science data into community water solutions (funded by the Mott Foundation) and Quantifying the interactive effects of human disturbances on lake biota and harmful algal blooms at the continental scale (funded by the National Science Foundation). This postdoc will be at the center of a new initiative turning 51 years of Michigan lake and stream data into tools and actions that communities can actually use. The position combines data science with community-engaged research, drawing on the LAGOS-US research platform ( www.lagoslakes.org ) and MiCorps, Michigan's statewide volunteer lake and stream monitoring program ( www.micorps.net ), to identify the stressors (e.g., nutrient pollution, invasive species, habitat loss, land-use change) shaping water quality across the state's agricultural, forested, and urban watersheds. In year one, the postdoc will also contribute to an update of one of the LAGOS-US data modules to support macroscale prediction of harmful algal blooms. Building on statewide analyses, the postdoc will help select six priority watersheds and partner directly with freshwater volunteers, watershed groups, municipal leaders, and resource managers to co-develop resources based on community needs (e.g., state-of-the-watershed reports, policy briefs, decision-making tools). These resources will help translate data into accessible, actionable information supporting locally driven conservation and stewardship. The role spans the full arc of data-intensive research and its application, and takes a community-engaged approach throughout. As a full collaborator with the research team, the postdoc will: co-create research questions; acquire and harmonize existing data and author metadata; visualize, analyze, and interpret data; review and synthesize literature; write and revise manuscripts, reports, and grant proposals as lead and co-author; publish code and data; and present results to scientific and community audiences alike. The successful candidate may also have the opportunity to supervise undergraduate students or on-call staff. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, citizenship, age, disability or protected veteran status. Required Degree Doctorate -ecology, aquatic ecology, limnology, or related discipline with strong quantitative emphasis (statistics and/or computer science). Minimum Requirements Doctorate (Ph.D.) in ecology, aquatic ecology, limnology, or related discipline with strong quantitative emphasis (statistics and/or computer science). Desired Qualifications Analytical skills and experience for conducting big-data lake ecology research using techniques from statistics and computer science (e.g., statistical modeling, machine learning, data mining). Knowledge of open science tools (e.g., Github, Zenodo). Demonstrated interest in conducting participatory or community-engaged research, including partnering with communities to develop research questions, interpret results, and apply learnings to management and conservation efforts by communities. Demonstrated capacity to conduct independent and collaborative research. A strong record of effective oral and written communication, including publishing in peer-reviewed journals and presenting scientific results to a variety of audiences. Excellent interpersonal skills including problem-solving, collaboration, and inclusion. Required Application Materials Please provide a 1-2 page cover letter that describes your research experiences and interests, a current CV, and names and contact information for three references. Special Instructions Expectations for in-person and remote work: MSU strives to provide a flexible work environment and this position has been designated as remote-friendly for Michigan residents. For this position, remote-friendly means the employee can regularly work 1-3 days per week away from MSU property. However, the postdoc will be expected to work in the Natural resources Building at least 2 days per week and will be expected to periodically attend in-person stakeholder meetings across Michigan to share data and gather input on co-developed resources. For additional information: Contact Dr. Jo Latimore ( latimor1@msu.edu ) or Dr. Kendra Spence Cheruvelil ( ksc@msu.edu ). Review of Applications Begins On 08/31/2026 Remote Work Statement MSU strives to provide a flexible work environment and this position has been designated as remote-friendly. Remote-friendly means some or all of the duties can be performed remotely as mutually agreed upon. Website https://www.canr.msu.edu/fw/ Department Statement The Department of Fisheries and Wildlife is a vibrant community of scholars including approximately 50 core faculty, 20 postdoctoral research associates, 100 graduate students, 240 undergraduate students, 7 core staff, and 25 research/center/program staff. Our Mission is to build local, national, and international capacities to conserve ecosystems that support fish, wildlife, and society through integrated programs in research, education and engagement. We are committed to the integrative nature of natural resources conservation and management, with our expertise ranging from disciplinary areas with a long-standing history in the domain of fisheries and wildlife to those emerging more recently. The Bridge lab at Michigan State University works to to advance inclusive and equity-focused solutions for broad-scale environmental challenges. We answer ecological questions at the intersections of many disciplines, which means that our research questions require knowledge and expertise from a diverse team. Because the theory, methods, and approaches to address these types of questions are sometimes lacking, we create new subdisciplines, build research infrastructure, and develop new approaches to conduct our work. MSU Statement Michigan State University has been advancing the common good with uncommon will for more than 160 years. One of the top research universities in the world, MSU pushes the boundaries of discovery and forges enduring partnerships to solve the most pressing global challenges while providing life-changing opportunities to a diverse and inclusive academic community through more than 200 programs of study in 17 degree-granting colleges. Apply Now Not Quite What You're Looking For? Sign Up For Job Alerts First Name (required) 02e7ca59 Last Name (required) 4157b6b1 Email (required) 22d9807e Job Alert 227988ef Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Fisheries And Wildlife 10002344 Fisheries And Wildlife 10002344 Categories Area of Interest: Research/Scientific Full Time/Part Time: Full Time (90-100%) Group: Fixed Term Academic Staff Remote Work: Remote-Friendly Union/Non-Union: Non-Union Area of Interest: Research/Scientific Full Time/Part Time: Full Time (90-100%) Group: Fixed Term Academic Staff Remote Work: Remote-Friendly Union/Non-Union: Non-Union Employment Type Faculty/Academic Staff Faculty/Academic Staff Locations East Lansing, Michigan, United States East Lansing, Michigan, United States Unit College Of Agriculture And Nat Resources College Of Agriculture And Nat Resources By clicking Send, you acknowledge that you are willing to receive SMS messages from Michigan State relating to the status of your application or other recruitment related information to any mobile / cell phone number entered in the form above. You can easily opt-out of receiving SMS messages by replying STOP to any messages you receive. Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Interested In Other Jobs At MSU? Check out these other recently posted roles that might be right for you! Executive Mgtmt Director-Exec Mgt East Lansing Msu It Campus It Experience 40000843 Join MSU IT where you’ll have the opportunity to influence enterprise-wide IT services at a leading public research university, shape the future of campus technology experiences, and lead teams that make a meaningful impact on students, faculty, staff, and the broader MSU community. Professional Aide Project Pay East Lansing Music 10030598 The College of Music offers an opportunity for classical musicians who are seeking to accompany other musicians and entertain our audiences with professional and thrilling performances Assistant/Associate Professor Tenure System East Lansing Psychology Social Science 10038698 The Department of Psychology at Michigan State University is recruiting a faculty member with expertise in industrial and organizational psychology or a